## Concept Overview

Blue-green deployment maintains two identical production environments, enabling zero-downtime deployments by routing traffic between them while Terraform manages infrastructure state and consistency.

## Implementation Details

- **Workspaces**: Create dedicated `blue` and `green` Terraform workspaces with identical infrastructure configurations
- **State Management**: Configure remote backend with state locking to prevent concurrent modifications and ensure state consistency
- **Atlantis Integration**: Set up PR-based workflows that deploy to the inactive workspace first, with automated validation and approval gates
- **Traffic Routing**: Implement load balancer target groups to seamlessly switch traffic after passing comprehensive health checks

## Code Example

```hcl
# workspace configuration
terraform {
  backend "remote" {
    organization = "your-org"
    workspaces {
      blue = "blue-prod"
      green = "green-prod"
    }
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This evaluates your ability to design a scalable drift-resilient Terraform workflow across multiple AWS accounts while ensuring safe automation and auditable infrastructure changes.

## Key Concepts
- Drift detection, automatic remediation, policy enforcement (Sentinel/OPA)
- Environment-specific remote backends, provider aliasing, central baseline state
- Module architecture boundaries, testing frameworks (Kitchen-Terraform/Terratest), CI/CD gates

## Code Example
```hcl
provider "aws" {
  alias  = "dev"
  region = "us-west-2"
}
terraform {
  required_providers {
    aws = {
      source  = "hashicorp/aws"
      version = "~> 5.0"
    }
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This tests how a junior engineer models read-only environments, data sourcing, and per-env state management without touching prod resources. It mirrors real-world patterns at scale at top tech companies.

## Key Concepts
- Terraform data sources for read-only references
- Per-env backends to segregate state
- Avoiding resource creation in isolated environments

## Code Example
```javascript
data "aws_vpc" "inventory" {
  filter {
    name   = "tag:Environment"
    values = ["inventory"]
  }
}
data "aws_subnet" "inventory" {
  filter {
    name   = "vpc-id"
    values = [data.aws_vpc.inventory.id]
  }
}
```

```javascript
terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket = "tf-state"
    key    = "inventory/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "us-east-1"
  }
}
```

```javascript
output "inventory_vpc_id" { value = data.aws_vpc.inventory.id }
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you verify that inventory plans never propose changes to prod data? 
- How would you automate inclusion of inventory in CI without accidentally applying prod changes?
